Interpretation
- 90 holder calls matched the exact return tasklet. The original lock survived native completion and return publication in all 90. The worker accepted the return and released the original lock from the stock different-object call entry site 0x144E614. All 90 then acquired a different object lock and dispatched FormList.GetAt.
- The waiting stack resumes its DynDOLOD script on the same Papyrus object after GetWorldSpace. The release comes at the following call to a different script object, not when the GetWorldSpace callback finishes or the scheduler finishes a list. Returning to the same owning object preserves its serialization until a genuine ownership change.
- No returned target missed an existing eligible tasklet collection. Publication to return acceptance was 0.2082 ms at median and 0.4720 ms at p95. From acceptance to release it was 0.0013 ms at median, 0.0044 ms at p95 and at most 0.0128 ms, without an intervening frame. That rules out a second tasklet-operation limit pause for this measured chain.
- The stock collector ran 2976 times, with zero job-array capacity increases and 0.0009 ms p95 elapsed time. No preallocation target was observed. The complete measured native marshalling cost for the 90 calls summed to 0.5348 ms across four runs, placing an upper bound on removing that specific computation.
- No supported acceleration candidate emerged from these three alternatives under the existing lock and FIFO constraints. The test DLL has observation only. The timings are stock baseline observations, not an A/B effect estimate. All four windows completed and PresentMon exited 0.

